# Provider credentials for Kilo agents (all optional).
# Only needed when you actually send prompts; the free Kilo Gateway works
# without any of these. Copy to `.env` and fill in, or pass them some other
# way (e.g. docker-compose.yml `environment:`). Any key kilo supports works.
KILO_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GOOGLE_API_KEY=

# NOTE: Kiloton's server variables (anything starting with KILOTON_*, plus
# KILO_BIN_PATH) are server-internal and are NOT passed to the spawned agents —
# only the provider keys above reach each agent process. Set server vars in the
# shell / compose `environment:` block, not here (e.g. KILOTON_PORT, KILOTON_HOST,
# KILOTON_CONFIG, KILOTON_NO_OPEN, KILOTON_NO_ORIGIN_CHECK).
#
# KILOTON_HOST trade-off:
#   Defaults to 127.0.0.1 (localhost only). Setting KILOTON_HOST=0.0.0.0 binds
#   on every interface and exposes the dashboard to your whole LAN — convenient
#   for reaching it from another device, but only do it on a trusted network.
#   By default the API and WebSocket are open to anyone who can reach the
#   server. Set KILOTON_TOKEN (below) to require a token on every HTTP request
#   and WebSocket upgrade before exposing the dashboard beyond a trusted network.
#
# KILOTON_TOKEN (enforced when set):
#   Optional API + WebSocket auth. When set, the server requires this secret on
#   every /api request and WebSocket upgrade. Accepted via the `Authorization`
#   header or a `?token=` query param (the WebSocket must use the query param,
#   since browsers can't set headers on the upgrade). Leave it unset for
#   local-only, no-auth use. Set it to safely expose the dashboard on a LAN with
#   KILOTON_HOST=0.0.0.0.
# Example:
#   KILOTON_TOKEN=change-me
# Usage:
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer change-me" http://localhost:7655/api/instances
#   ws://localhost:7655?token=change-me
